meta {
  name: Fetch one link - not found
  type: http
  seq: 13
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/links/00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000000
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

tests {
  test("answers 404 rather than confirming or denying anything else", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(404);
    expect(res.body.error.code).to.equal("not_found");
  });
}

docs {
  FR-002's "indistinguishable by design", from the reading side.

  This id belongs to no link at all. Another account's id — `riv4l99`'s, say —
  produces a byte-identical response: the same 404, the same `not_found`, the
  same message. That is the point, and it is why the ownership scope lives in
  the query rather than in a branch after it. A 403 would confirm that the id
  names a real link, which is exactly the fact the caller is not entitled to.

  The pair is asserted in
  `backend/spec/requests/api/v1/links_isolation_spec.rb`, which compares the two
  response bodies directly — something this collection cannot do, because
  nothing here knows the rival's link id and giving it one would mean adding an
  endpoint that hands it over.

  A soft-deleted link of the caller's own answers the same way (FR-028): the row
  and its statistics survive, but it is gone from the API.
}
